Uncertain resource transfer retention
====================================

Run resource_transfer_test.sh and overlay_retention_test.sh with sh. They
extract the actual controller senders and overlay lifetime functions. Transport,
native resource operations and kernfs are controlled boundaries. No test here
boots a kernel, runs a delayed remote worker or proves PCI/DMA quiescence.

All six CPU/memory/device controller senders require mk_overlay_mutex, as do
their overlay callers and Image operations. A successful ring enqueue is
publication; transport errors currently occur before a slot is published.
Cancelling a pending waiter does not cancel a published request.

Memory and device ownership must be reserved before publication. Device add
also reserves ownership before admitting config access. Before publication,
allocation or send errors undo the reservation; failed cleanup retains it and
reports the cleanup error. After publication, any unsuccessful wait retains
ownership and marks the instance resource_uncertain. A negative ACK cannot
certify rollback: the receiver can have changed one memory piece, failed the
next, and failed to restore the first. A timeout permits later worker execution.
Memory removal therefore keeps its range reserved against Image allocations;
device failures retain lending and do not reset a potentially busy function.
The free-pool device-add path checks all other instance grants before touching
the local function. Otherwise a second overlay could make a retained function
assignable again without addressing the quarantined instance at all.

The flag blocks further CPU/memory/device controller changes, start, Image
reuse/unload and destruction through the common confirmation gate. Freeing all
instance memory also checks it. Even confirmed stopped CPUs cannot prove a
resource rollback or DMA settlement. There is no automatic flag clearing:
recovery requires rebooting the controller. Graceful/force halt can still request
that CPUs stop, but settlement returns -EBUSY and does not publish LOADED.
This deliberately sacrifices availability, including for a negative ACK that
happened before any remote change. A future recovery protocol must distinguish
that case and prove all admitted resource work has retired before granting reuse.

Overlay records are allocated and populated while inactive, before resource
operations begin. Record allocation failure cannot strand applied changes.
An apply failure can leave a partially applied transaction; it remains visible
as failed. rmdir only inverses an applied transaction, and the first inverse
failure permanently marks it failed. Repeating a failed inverse could undo
operations that never ran, or undo an already-reversed item twice. The unused
overlay exit/discard path was removed; it could free records after failed
rollback and before draining attribute readers. No per-operation transaction
journal or automatic retry is provided. Successful removal still drains kernfs
readers outside mk_overlay_mutex before freeing the transaction.

Coverage: negative ACKs and timeouts for all four memory/device operations,
ownership before lending/publication, competing reservations, attempts to
reinsert a retained PCI grant through the free-pool path, unpublished error
cleanup, cleanup failures, resource quarantine across state changes, metadata
allocation failure before overlay effects, partial apply/inverse and repeated
removal. cpu_ownership_test, memory_teardown_test and start_lifetime_test exercise
the actual reuse gates. image_lock_test checks controller serialization with
real host mutexes; its transaction begins in APPLIED state.

Remaining qualification: actual delayed/partial receiver work, live lockdep,
hardware PCI reset/DMA and native/QEMU resource cycles. Old PCI requests still
in a shared ring need a separate generation/admission contract; pinning a
queued worker or retaining a grant does not establish that contract.
